INT. TIDE MILL MUSEUM - NIGHT

A great timber wheel hangs motionless in a dry race. Pale moonlight leaks through slatted windows and lays long bars across the floor. Millstones the size of cartwheels sit in their cases like fallen moons.

Five figures in dark clothes drop one by one from a skylight on ropes. MADAME ODILE FONTAINE, 60s, the planner, lands first, soundless, silver hair under a black scarf. FENWICK PRIDDY, 50s, the safecracker, follows, wheezing. LAKSHMI IYER, 30s, the electrician, lands lightly. ULRICH BAUMANN, 40s, the muscle, thuds down last and winces at the noise. And DELILAH MWANGI, 30s, the inside woman, steps out of the shadows in a museum fleece, a lanyard around her neck.

ODILE
(to the group)
Positions.

They scatter without a word. Lakshmi to the fuse box. Ulrich to the stairwell.

FENWICK
(muttering)
Forty years since I did a window.

ODILE
(to Fenwick)
You did it last month.

FENWICK
That was a different window.

LAKSHMI
Power's mine in ninety seconds.

DELILAH
(to Odile)
The case is on the second floor. Under the gears.

ODILE
And the guard?

DELILAH
Asleep. I put something in his tea.

ODILE
(low)
Good girl.

They climb. Wooden stairs complain under each step. On the second floor, behind a cluster of cogs, a glass case holds a bronze tide-clock the size of a loaf.

Fenwick kneels. Puts his stethoscope to the lock. Closes his eyes.

FENWICK
Come on, sweetheart.

LAKSHMI (O.S.)
(from below, a stage whisper)
Lights in five.

ODILE
(to everyone)
Hold your breath.

The mill goes black. In the dark, a click. A second click. The case glass lifts.

Fenwick lifts the clock out with both hands. Cradles it like an infant.

FENWICK
Got her.

A beam of white light sweeps the room. The guard in the doorway, rubbing his eyes. A thermos in his hand. Everyone freezes.

FRANK (O.S.)
(slurred)
Delilah?

Delilah steps forward, into the light, smiling.

DELILAH
Frank. You should be asleep.

FRANK (O.S.)
I was. Had a dream.

She crosses to him. Slips an arm round his shoulder. Turns him around.

DELILAH
Back to bed, Frank.

He lets himself be steered. Odile exhales. The beam retreats. Ulrich's fist unclenches.

Fenwick tucks the clock into a velvet bag. They file toward the skylight, silent and fast.

Last up the rope, Odile glances back at the great wheel. She touches two fingers to her lips and presses them to the timber as she passes.
